  <static seed="56" source="Library/Michigan/Chap3Sec2/Q39.pg">
    <statement><p>Certain pieces of antique furniture increased very rapidly in price in
the 1970s and 1980s.  For example, the value of a particular rocking
chair is well approximated by 
<me>V = 70 (1.6)^t,</me>
where  <m>V</m>  is in dollars
and  <m>t</m> is the number of years since 1975.  Find the rate, in dollars
per year, at which the price is increasing.</p>
<p>rate = <fillin characters="35" name="AnSwEr0001"/> dollars/yr</p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>70\ln\mathopen{}\left(1.6\right)\cdot 1.6^{t}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><me>\hbox{rate} = 70 \ln(1.6) 1.6^t \quad\hbox{dollars/year}.</me>


</solution>
